## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of industrial society, large amounts of dyes are released into rivers and oceans during production and service processes. Due to their high solubility, high persistence, high carcinogenicity, and mutagenicity, dyes in water-bodies have caused many environmental problems, which lead to serious threats to the health and safety of human beings, animals, and plants [1-3]. Thus, the removal of dyes from wastewater has attracted considerable attention. Compared with other reported methods, photocatalytic degradation is considered one of the most promising methods for the dye removal owing to its advantages of simple operation, nontoxicity, and high efficiency [4-6]. Hence, it is necessary to develop excellent photocatalysts for dye removal.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), formed by metal nodes and organic ligands, are an emerging class of porous materials with high porosity and large surface area, which show great potential for gas adsorption and storage, biosensors, and catalysis [7-9]. In particular, increasing interest has been focused on the application of photocatalysis in recent years [10-14]. Notably, among the reported MOFs, Ti-based MOFs, represented by MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (MIL for Materials of Institut Lavoisier), have been widely applied in photocatalysis owing to their small bandgap energy, wide light-absorption range, high porosity, and high content of titanium centers [15-19]. However, the microporous structure restricts the entry of larger molecules into the MOF channel, thus limiting the application of MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ in dye photodegradation. The electronic structure of MOFs also needs to be further adjusted to achieve better catalytic activity.
Considering the catalytic mechanism of MOFs, photocatalysis by MOFs depends on two aspects: the efficiency of free radical production and the utilization of free radicals in the photocatalytic process. To date, various strategies have been explored to enhance the inherent ability of semiconductors to generate free radicals, such as facet engineering, morphology regulation, and forming heterojunction structures [20-23]. The introduction of defect structures, especially oxygen vacancies (OVs), into photocatalysts has attracted much attention [24-26]. First, the existence of OVs can facilitate the migration of charge carriers and inhibit electronhole recombination [27]; second, the unsaturated coordination sites derived from OVs can provide more active sites for molecule adsorption and catalysis [28]. Thirdly, OVs may improve charge transfer to the defect level, which promotes the surface catalytic reaction [26]. Last but not least, the OVs could promote the adsorption of visible light, thereby narrowing the bandgap [29]. Therefore, the introduction of OVs into photocatalysts is an effective method of improving the generation of free radicals, thereby enhancing the photocatalytic efficiency to facilitate the catalytic reaction. Although the introduction of structural defects into MOFs has been widely reported, the contribution of OVs in MOFs to the catalysis of reactions has been rarely studied, especially for photocatalytic processes.
In addition, the efficiency of utilization of the photogenerated radicals for promoting the catalytic reaction has rarely been studied. To the best of our knowledge, the free radicals generated in photocatalysis have very short lifetimes of only $10^{-6}$ to $10^{-9}$ s and a limited diffusion distance [30,31]. Note that some of the radicals are quenched before the photocatalytic reaction occurs; thus, the photo-generated radicals cannot be fully utilized [32]. Therefore, shortening the spatial distance between the active sites and substrates is essential. In this regard, the introduction of mesopores can resolve this problem to some extent. The existence of mesopores can enlarge the pore size of MOFs and allow large organic molecules to enter the pore channel, which increases the probability of contact between the radicals and pollutant molecules, thus enhancing the catalytic activity [33-35]. More importantly, the adsorption-induced enrichment effect of hierarchical pores in MOFs can ensure a short distance between the organic molecules and active sites, allowing efficient utilization of the photo-generated free radicals in the confined space of MOFs [36- 39].
In recent years, many methods of introducing mesoporous structures into MOFs have been reported, especially the ligandextension strategy, in which extended linkers are utilized to increase the pore size of MOFs [40,41]. However, the preparation of longer organic ligands is complex and time-consuming. In addition, the extended frameworks of MOFs are relatively fragile, undergoing rapid collapse after the removal of guest molecules [42,43]. In contrast, hierarchically porous MOFs (H-MOFs) with both microporous and mesoporous structures have attracted increasing attention owing to the combined advantages of microporous MOFs and mesoporous MOFs [44-48]. Although the template method has been widely adopted to prepare H-MOFs [49,50], utilizing a large number of template agents in the reaction system greatly reduces the crystallinity of the MOFs and damages the original crystal morphology [51,52]. In contrast, the morphology and structural integrity of the MOF crystals can be maintained in HMOFs prepared by the post-synthesis method [53,54].
Inspired by the above considerations, in order to improve both the inherent ability of MOFs to generate free radicals and the utilization of the generated radicals, we propose a strategy that simultaneously introduces OVs and hierarchically porous structures into MOFs to enhance the photocatalytic degradation of dyes. As a proof-of-concept experiment, propionic acid (PA) is adopted as the etching agent to prepare a single-crystal MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ nanoreactor with both OVs and hierarchical pore structures, as the photocatalyst, denoted as $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}_{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0},$ . After treatment with PA, the crystallinity and morphology of the material remain intact. Compared with conventional strong acid etching, monocarboxylic acid is relatively mild, and it is easy to control the etching degree. ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption and electron spin resonance (ESR) analyses confirm the successful introduction of hierarchical pores and OV structures in H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ . Optical electrochemical analysis demonstrates that H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ absorbs over an extended visible light range, has a narrower bandgap, and facilitates carrier migration. As expected, due to the synergistic effect of the OVs and hierarchical pores, $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ exhibits excellent photocatalytic performance for the removal of rhodamine (RhB), and the catalytic rate constant of H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ is approximately eight times larger than that of pristine MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . Analysis of the mechanism reveals that the active species (OH, $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ , and $\mathbf{e}^{-}$ are the primary contributors to the photocatalytic process. This work highlights the important roles of OVs and hierarchical pore structures in improving the photocatalytic performance owing to the enhanced generation and utilization of free radicals.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
All chemicals were commercially available. Titanium isopropoxide $\mathrm{'}C_{12}\mathrm{H}_{29}0_{4}\mathrm{Ti}$ , $97\%$ ) was purchased from Macklin. 2- Aminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{\tilde{NH}}_{2}$ -BDC, $98\%$ ) and benzoquinone (BQ, $98\%$ were supplied by TCI. RhB (AR) was purchased from Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. Acetone $(\geq99.5\%)$ was supplied by Tianjin Fengchuan Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF, $\geq99.5\%$ ), methanol $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}0\mathrm{H},\ \geq99.5\%)$ acetic acid $\mathrm{\cdot}C H_{3}C O O H$ , $\geq99.5\%$ , PA $(\geq99.5\%)$ , dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $299.5\%$ , isopropanol (IPA, $299.7\%$ , and butyric acid (BA, $299.5\%)$ were obtained from Sinopharm Group Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. DMF was dried over $4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ molecular sieves, and $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H}$ was treated with Na, distilled, and stored on $4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ molecular sieves before the experiment.
## 2.2. Material preparation
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of $M I L-125–N H_{2}$
$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC $(2.70\ \mathrm{g})$ was dissolved in a mixture of DMF $25~\mathrm{mL}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ ( $25~\mathrm{mL}$ ; $\mathrm{C}_{12}\mathrm{H}_{29}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}$ $3~\mathrm{mL}$ ) was added to the mixture and stirred for $2\ \mathrm{min}$ . After mixing the two solutions, $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ $(5~\mathrm{mL}$ ) was added. The resulting mixture was placed into a Teflon reactor, heated to $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , and maintained at this temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the obtained solid was washed by immersion in DMF, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , and acetone for $_{3\mathrm{~d~}}$ . The final samples were dried in an oven at $60^{\circ}C$ .
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of $H_{-}M I L-125–N H_{2}–V_{O}$
MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $(0.20\ \mathrm{g})$ was added to the PA solution $(13.4\mathrm{M})$ in a $70~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined steel autoclave. The mixture was heated to $90~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The product was centrifuged, washed in deionized (DI) water and acetone three times, and finally dried at $60~^{\circ}C$ in an oven.
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of $M I L–125–N H_{2}–V_{O}$
MIL-125- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ $(0.20\ \mathrm{g})$ was added to the PA solution $(10.0\mathrm{M})$ in a $70~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined steel autoclave. The mixture was heated to $90^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The product was treated in a manner similar to that in the above experiment.
## 2.3. Characterization
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were recorded on a Bruker AXS D2 PHASER instrument. The X-ray powder diffractometer was equipped with a Cu sealed tube $(\mathrm{k}=1.54178\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) that was kept at room temperature. Scans were performed in the 2h range of $2{-}50^{\circ}$ , with a step size of $0.04^{\circ}$ . Nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms and aperture distribution measurements at $77~\mathrm{K}$ were performed on BeiShiDe 3H-2000PS2 and Micrometrics ASAP 2020 PLUS HD 88 instruments. UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (UVDRS) were recorded on a Hitachi UH4150 instrument. UV–vis spectra were acquired using a SHIMADZU UV-2600 instrument. Photoluminescence (PL) measurements were performed using a Hitachi F-7000 apparatus. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and photocurrent tests were performed using a CHI760 electrochemical workstation. ESR measurements were performed on a Bruker A300 instrument.
## 2.4. Evaluation of photocatalytic activity
The photocatalytic degradation of RhB was carried out under visible-light irradiation from a 300 W Xe lamp with a UV cut-off filter $(\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm})$ ). MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ or H-MIL-125- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}{-}{\mathsf{V}}_{0}$ $_{(0.020\ \mathrm{g})}$ was added to $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of RhB $(250~\mathrm{mg/L})$ . Prior to irradiation, the adsorption–desorption equilibrium between RhB and the catalyst was reached by keeping the mixture in the dark for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The solution was then exposed to visible light with stirring. At regular intervals, $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of the solution was extracted and filtered using a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ filter. The concentration of RhB in the supernatant solution was evaluated using a UV–vis spectrophotometer.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structural characterization
ESR measurements were performed to corroborate the generation of OVs in $H_{-}\mathbf{MIL}-125\mathbf{-NH}_{2}\mathbf{-V}_{0}$ . As shown in Fig. 2a, a symmetrical signal was observed at $g=2.003$ in the ESR spectra of all samples, indicating the existence of OVs [28]. Notably, the signal of the OVs was more intense in the case of the H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ and MIL-125- ${\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{V}}_{0}$ samples compared to the untreated samples, and that of H-MIL-125- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ was the strongest, which suggests that the concentration of OVs depends on the etching degree. In addition, the $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ signal was detected at $g=1.944$ for all samples; this is attributed to the existence of OVs, which induces charge transfer [18,56]. In the Ti 2p core level XPS spectra of H-MIL$125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ (Fig. 2b), two bands were observed at 457.6 eV (Ti $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2},$ and $463.4\mathrm{eV}$ (Ti $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ ), which are obviously less intense than those of the pristine MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (457.8 and $463.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ ). In comparison, the Ti $\mathsf{2p}$ peaks of MIL- ${\cdot125–\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{V}_{0}}$ were slightly shifted to 457.7 and 463.5 eV [20,57]. This reveals that the copious generation of OVs in H-MIL-125- $-N\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}$ can tune the electronic state of the Ti sites, which means that the optoelectronic properties and photocatalytic activity can also be adjusted. These results clearly demonstrate that both hierarchically porous structures and OVs were successfully introduced into PA-treated H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ .
To investigate the thermal stability of the etched MOF, pristine MIL-125- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , MIL- $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left.125{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{-}\mathrm{V}_{0},\aftergroup\egroup\right.$ , and H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ were subjected to thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). The weight loss of all samples before $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ can be attributed to the removal of solvent molecules from the pores (Fig. S7). Above $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , all three MOFs began to decompose, suggesting that the thermal stability of H$M_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{IL-}125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ is similar to that of pristine MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ .
## 3.2. Optoelectronic properties
To explore the effect of OVs on the optical response of H-MIL$125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ , M $1\mathrm{IL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{V}_{0}$ , and MIL- ${\cdot125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ , UV–vis DRS measurements were carried out (Fig. 3a). In comparison to pristine $\mathsf{M I L}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ with an adsorption edge of $480\ \mathrm{nm}$ , the peaks of HM $\mathrm{IIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}{-}\mathrm{V}_{0}$ and MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ were obviously red-shifted and the adsorption edges were further extended due to the localized mid-gap states in the bandgap and the greater number of defects; $H_{-}\mathbf{MIL}-125\mathbf{-NH}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ displayed an adsorption maximum at approximately $392{\mathrm{nm}}$ , with an adsorption edge of $560\mathrm{nm}$ , which extends the adsorption of visible light. Based on the Kubelka-Munk equation: $(\mathsf{a h}\nu)^{2}=\mathsf{A}(\mathsf{h}\nu-E_{g})$ the calculated bandgaps of these materials can be arranged in the following order: MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $(2.77~\mathrm{{\textperthousand}})~>~{\mathsf{M I L}}\mathsf{-}125\mathrm{{\–NH}_{2}\mathrm{{-}V_{0}}}$ $(2.62\mathrm{\Large~\eV})\ >\ \mathrm{H-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-V_{0}}$ $(2.56\mathrm{eV})$ , indicating that the incorporation of defect structures into the MOF contributes to bridging the bandgap (Fig. 3b). To determine the energy-level alignment in the bandgap structure of the MOFs, Mott-Schottky electrochemical measurements were conducted. The positive slope of the Mott-Schottky plots (Figs. S8– S10) indicates that $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}_{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ is a typical $n$ -type semiconductor [58]. The flat potentials determined from the intersection were evaluated to be $-0.82,\thinspace-0.79$ , and $-0.71\ \mathrm{eV}$ versus $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ for MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{M I L}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ , and $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}_{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ respectively. It is generally accepted that the bottom of the conduction band (CB) in most semiconductors is approximately equal to the flat-band potential. Hence, the CB edge of H-MIL-125- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{V}_{0}$ was estimated to be $-0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ versus the normal hydrogen electrode (NHE), which is lower than that of pristine MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (– $0.21~\mathrm{eV})$ and MIL- $125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}$ VO $(-0.18\ \mathrm{eV})$ . Moreover, the intrinsic charge carrier density $(N_{d})$ is an important parameter for estimating the catalytic activity, where a higher value represents better catalytic performance and is inversely proportional to the slope of the linear part of the Mott-Schottky plot. The slope of the Mott-Schottky plot was smallest for $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ , which suggests that the numerous OV structures remarkably increased the $N_{d}$ of the MOFs, suggesting better photocatalytic performance. Based on the bandgap and CB values, the corresponding valence band (VB) levels were calculated to be 2.56, 2.52, and $2.46\mathrm{eV}$ versus NHE for MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , MIL- ${\cdot125{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N H}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{V}_{0}}$ , and H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{O}},$ , respectively.
The rate of separation of the photogenerated charge carriers plays an important role in enhancing the photocatalytic activity. PL measurements were performed to explore the effect of the hierarchically porous structure and OVs of the MOFs on the transfer efficiency of the photo-induced charge carriers (Fig. S11). When the hierarchically porous structure and OVs were introduced into MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , the PL emission intensity became weaker. The PL intensity was lowest for $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}_{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0},$ indicating that the simultaneous incorporation of OVs and hierarchically porous structures could slow the rate of recombination of the electrons and holes, as well as enhance the separation rate, which beneficially influences the photocatalytic activity. The separation efficiency of the photogenerated electron-hole pairs was also evaluated via photocurrent response measurements. Fig. 3c shows the photocurrent response spectra of the samples under visiblelight irradiation for on/off cycles. The photocurrent signal of HMIL- ${\cdot125–N\mathrm{H}_{2}–\mathrm{V}_{0}}$ is approximately two times more intense than that of MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , whereas that of MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ was only enhanced 1.4-fold. This means that the defect structures could accelerate the separation of photogenerated electron-hole pairs [10]. The EIS measurements further verified this deduction (Fig. 3d). The arc radius in the EIS Nyquist plot reflects the interfacial resistance of the charge transfer. The order of the radii was as follows: MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}}>\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-NH_{2}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{V_{0}}>\mathrm{H}.$ -MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ . As the number of defect structures increased, the radii become increasingly smaller, suggesting that H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ has the lowest charge transfer resistance and the fastest migration rate. Based on the above results, it can be inferred that the introduction of defect structures, including OVs and hierarchical pores, can not only modulate the coordination structures and electronic states through the surface dangling bonds and localized electrons [25], but can also extend the light response to the visible/IR region by narrowing the bandgap of semiconductors [29], which can greatly facilitate the generation of charge carriers. On the other hand, defect structures can function as electron-trapping sites. When the electrons are captured by defect sites, they may be consumed by the adsorbed reactants before recombining with the holes, thus inhibiting charge carrier recombination and enhancing charge transfer to the defect level [59]. Therefore, the photocatalytic activity of H-MIL- $125\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH_{2}}}\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{V}}$ O was efficiently promoted when OVs and hierarchical pores were created.
## 3.3. Photocatalytic degradation of RhB
To confirm the superior photocatalytic activity of H-MIL-125- ${\sf N H}_{2}–\mathsf{V}_{0}$ , the photodegradation of RhB was evaluated (Fig. 4a). Control tests were performed in the dark before illumination. The dye concentration in the test system remained unchanged after $24\mathrm{h}$ of dark treatment, indicating that the adsorption–desorption equilibrium of RhB was achieved. The RhB removal efficiency of MIL-125- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ under dark conditions was calculated to be only $13\%$ and that of MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ increased to $23\%$ . In comparison, for H-MIL$125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ , the removal efficiency reached $52\%$ . The tests show that $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}_{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ has better ability to adsorb RhB because the hierarchical pores in the MOF can enhance access of RhB molecules to the pore channel. To explore the indispensable roles of light and the photocatalyst in the photocatalytic RhB degradation process, control experiments were carried out by simultaneous exposure to light and catalyst, as well as without light and without catalyst, respectively, as shown in Fig. S12. These results suggest that illumination and photocatalyst are preconditions for the photocatalytic degradation of RhB.
Therefore, after dark treatment, the catalytic degradation of RhB under visible-light illumination was evaluated using MIL- ${\cdot125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ , M $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{IL}{-}125{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{-}\mathrm{V}_{0},}\end{array}$ , and H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ under the same irradiation conditions. Fig. 4b shows the color change and UV–vis spectra of RhB in the photocatalytic reaction using H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ . As the photoirradiation time increased, the RhB concentration decreased, and the color changed from pink to nearly colorless. In addition, the absorption maximum of RhB at $553~\mathrm{nm}$ became less intense with increasing contact time, which can be ascribed to RhB removal. The percentage degradation of RhB in the presence of $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ reached $99.0\%$ , whereas that achieved with MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ was only $64\%$ , as evaluated from the degradation effect plot, and both values were much higher than that of MIL
$125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . To further understand the reaction kinetics of RhB degradation over the catalyst samples, the experimental data for RhB degradation were fitted by the pseudo-first-order kinetic model $(\ln(C_{0}/C)=\mathbf{k}t)$ , as presented in Fig. 4c. The corresponding fitting results are presented in Table S1. The obtained reaction rate constant of $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}_{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ was approximately eight times higher than that of pristine MIL- ${\cdot125–\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ , while that for MIL- ${\cdot125–\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{V}_{0}}$ was only 2.7 times higher. The above results show that H-MIL-125- ${\sf N H}_{2}–\mathsf{V}_{0}$ exhibits high adsorption capacity and excellent photocatalytic activity, and the total degradation percentage of RhB reached $99.0\%$ during the overall degradation process. The enhanced photocatalytic activity can be attributed to the following points: (1) the introduction of hierarchical pores boosts the molecule transfer rate and improves the adsorption capacity; (2) the generation of the OV structure reduces the bandgap and increases the rate of charge carrier migration; (3) under the synergistic effect of the OVs and hierarchically porous structures, RhB is enriched in the MOF and can make sufficient contact with the active sites; thus, many free radicals can be efficiently utilized, thereby enhancing the photocatalytic performance. The efficiency of the photocatalyst, the fast mass transfer rate, strong ability to generate free radicals, and efficient utilization of free radicals make H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ an efficient photocatalyst.
To explore whether the defect structure of $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ was identical before and after the catalytic reaction, the XRD patterns, $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms, and ESR spectra of the samples were acquired before and after the photocatalytic reaction, as shown in Fig. S13. There were no significant changes in the purity of the crystal phase, mesopores, and OVs for the HMIL-125- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}{-}\mathsf{V}_{0}$ samples before and after the reaction, indicating that the defect structure of $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ was preserved in the photocatalytic reaction.
Moreover, a photocatalytic recycling experiment was performed to study the stability and reusability of H-MIL- ${\cdot125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ - $\mathsf{V}_{0}$ . After each cycle, the samples were centrifuged and washed before the next photocatalytic experiment under the same conditions. After recycling five times, the percentage degradation of the material remained unchanged, indicating that $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ could be reused multiple times (Fig. S14). The RhB degradation efficiency of H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ in real water samples was assessed using river water and lake water. The degradation efficiency of RhB in river water and lake water exceeded $95\%$ after $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ of irradiation (Fig. S15), which is slightly lower than that in DI water. This is because there are many inorganic anions in real water environments, such as $\subset{1-}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , and $C0_{3}^{-}$ , which have a certain degree of influence on the catalytic performance [60]. Despite this influence, $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}_{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ exhibited high degradation efficiency. The above results confirm that H-M $1\mathrm{IL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{-}\mathrm{V}_{0}$ is a promising photocatalyst for wastewater treatment. To explore the major active species in the photocatalytic reaction process, radical trapping experiments were carried out by adding diverse scavengers to the photocatalytic system. In this study, BQ, IPA, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , and DMSO were used as scavengers of $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ , OH, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ , and $\mathsf{e}^{-}$ , respectively [61,62]. Fig. 4d shows the degradation efficiency of RhB in the presence of different radical scavengers. Compared with the blank experiment, the degradation efficiency decreased from $99.0\%$ to $52.8\%$ after the addition of BQ, indicating that $\cdot_{\mathbf{0}_{2}^{-}}$ exerts a predominant impact on the catalytic reaction. Considering the possible overlap of the absorption of BQ with that of RhB at $553\mathrm{nm}$ in aqueous solution, we compared the UV–vis spectra of RhB, BQ, and their mixtures, as shown in Fig. S16. The absorption peak of BQ was observed at $246\mathrm{nm}$ and there was no absorbance at $553\mathrm{nm}$ . When BQ was added to the RhB solution, the absorbance of the mixed solution at $553~\mathrm{nm}$ did not change compared with that of the pure RhB solution. Notably, $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ can also act as an $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ scavenger [59]. As shown in Fig. S17, the degradation efficiency decreased to $53.6\%$ after adding ${\sf N}_{2}$ to the photocatalytic system, which is close to the degradation efficiency when BQ acts as an $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ trapping agent. These results suggest that the adsorption of BQ has little effect on the degradation efficiency and demonstrate that the results achieved with BQ as an $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ scavenger are credible. Similarly, when IPA was added to trap OH, the photocatalytic activity declined, and the removal efficiency decreased to $62.8\%$ , indicating that OH also plays an important role in the reaction process. In the presence of DMSO, the RhB degradation efficiency was reduced to $69.0\%$ , indicating that $\mathsf{e}^{-}$ plays a role in the photocatalytic degradation process. Nevertheless, the degradation efficiency decreased to $93.4\%$ with the addition of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , demonstrating that $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ has only a limited contribution to RhB degradation and that $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ is not a major active species. These results reveal that $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ , OH, and $\mathsf{e}^{-}$ are the major active species in the photocatalytic process, among which, the contributions of $\cdot_{\mathbf{0}_{2}^{-}}$ and OH are outstanding.
ESR measurements were utilized to further prove the active species (OH $,\cdot0_{2}^{-},\mathrm{h}^{+},\mathrm{e}^{-})$ produced by the photocatalyst, by using 5,5-dimethyl-1-pyrroline $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -oxide (DMPO) and 2,2,6,6-tetrame thyl-1-piperidinyloxy (TEMPO) as capture agents [63,64]. As illustrated in Fig. 5a, the signal of ${\tt D M P O-}^{.}{\tt O}_{2}^{-}$ could not be detected in the dark experiment. When exposed to light for $2~\mathrm{min}$ , the signal of DMPO- $\cdot_{\mathbf{0}_{2}^{-}}$ was observed, and the signal intensity increased significantly when the photoirradiation time was extended. Similarly, the DMPO-OH signal was also only observed under light illumination and became stronger over time (Fig. 5b). In addition, the TEMPO trapping agent can be oxidized to $\mathrm{TEMPO^{+}}$ by the photogenerated holes and reduced to TEMPOH by the electrons; thus, the signal of TEMPO becomes weaker. As shown in Fig. 5c, the signal of TEMPO- $\cdot\mathbf{e}^{-}$ gradually weakened with prolonged illumination and completely disappeared at 6 min. The signal of TEMPO- $\cdot\mathrm{h}^{+}$ also decreased over time, but some signals could still be detected after $6\mathrm{{min}}$ (Fig. 5d). In contrast, the photoelectrons produced in H-MIL$125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ have a strong ability to produce photoelectrons under light; these electrons can completely redox TEMPO. The ESR data show that the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{e}}^{-}$ , OH, and $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ active species are generated in the photocatalytic degradation process, which is in accordance with the results of the radical trapping experiment. Moreover, compared with pristine MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ , the signals of DMPO-OH and ${\tt D M P O-}^{\cdot}{\tt O}_{2}^{-}$ were distinctly strengthened in the reaction employing $H-M\mathrm{IL}-125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-V}_{0},$ whereas that of TEMPO$\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ lost intensity and the signal of TEMPO- $\cdot\mathrm{e}^{-}$ disappeared upon exposure of the system to light (Figs. S18–S21), further confirming that the defective $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ possesses excellent photocatalytic activity.
Considering the results discussed above, the proposed photodegradation process under $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ catalysis is presented in Scheme 2. As shown, owing to the relatively narrow band structure, $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ can be excited to induce electronic transition to the CB, thus leaving holes in the VB under visible light irradiation. The existence of OVs in $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ can significantly facilitate the generation of free radicals. This can broaden the light adsorption range, trap electrons, and greatly reduce the recombination rate of charge carriers, thus accelerating the carrier migration rate. Moreover, the VB potential is higher than $E(\cdot{\mathrm{OH}}/{\mathrm{OH}}^{-})$ $\left(+1.99\mathrm{eV}\right.$ vs. NHE) [65], which increases the yield of OH radicals through the oxidation of adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules by $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ . Meanwhile, $\mathrm{e^{-}}$ can reduce the dissolved oxygen to produce $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ radicals because the CB potential is more negative than $E$ $(0_{2}/\cdot0_{2}^{-})$ $(^{-}0.046\ \mathrm{eV}$ vs. NHE) [66]. As a result, $\cdot0_{2}^{-},$ OH, and $\mathsf{e}^{-}$ are the main active species in the photocatalytic process that simultaneously degrade RhB, as confirmed by the trap experiments and ESR measurements. The major reaction steps for RhB degradation are represented by the following equations:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{MIL}-125-\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{V}_{0}+\mathrm{h}_{\mathrm{v}}}\\ &{\quad\rightarrow\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{MIL}-125-\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{V}_{0}(\mathrm{h}^{+}+\mathrm{e}^{-})}\\ &{\quad}\\ &{\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}}\\ &{\quad-\mathrm{OH}+\mathrm{h}^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{OH}}\\ &{\quad\mathrm{RhB}+\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}/\cdot\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{product}}\end{array}
$$
Therefore, the introduction of both hierarchical pores and OV structures to promote the catalytic activity of $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}_{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ was demonstrated. On one hand, the structure of the OVs in HMIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{(}}$ O can greatly increase the free radical yield and enhance the efficiency of electron-hole separation. On the other hand, the presence of hierarchical pores in $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ not only leads to pore enrichment for RhB adsorption, but also shortens the distance between the active site and RhB, facilitating contact of RhB with the photogenerated radicals, thereby greatly enhancing the utilization of free radicals. The above analysis demonstrates that the synergistic effect of OVs and hierarchically porous structures can effectively degrade RhB.
## 4. Conclusion
In general, hierarchical porous H-MIL- ${\cdot125–\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{V}_{0}}$ with an OV structure was prepared through a facile and controlled acid etching process, in which PA served as an etching agent. Compared with pristine MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , H-MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{C}}$ exhibited a large surface area, rich micro-mesoporous structure, and excellent optoelectronic properties. Owing to the introduction of OVs, H-MIL$125\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}V_{0}}$ promoted carrier migration and generated more radicals upon exposure to light. Significantly, the hierarchical pore structure can facilitate mass transfer, and the adsorption-induced pore enrichment effect enables adequate utilization of free radicals. Thus, the combined contributions of hierarchical pores and OV structures greatly enhance the catalytic activity. The results show that the catalytic rate constant of $H_{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}125\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{V}_{0}$ for RhB degradation was eight times higher than that of pristine MIL- ${\cdot125–\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ . Although the effect of OVs on photocatalysis has been studied in many metal oxides [24,26,67], the influence of introducing OVs into MOFs on photocatalysis is reported here for the first time. Notably, this work not only reports a promising photocatalyst for the treatment of wastewater, but also emphasizes the important role of hierarchical pores and OVs of MOFs in photocatalytic reactions.
